{"id":"deferrals","ord":"a0","title":"Deferrals","desc":"NO DEFERRALS (the human, 2026-08-22): a PR ships everything its item names and everything it spots in touched code; what cannot be done STOPS and ASKS. The Inbox holds only what the human chooses to park. ONE exception: a test found flaky on master that a PR does not own is NOT a deferral — it goes to the Inbox's top-level `flaky tests` ledger, governed by dag-pr-ci's FLAKE POLICY.","created":"2026-08-21T17:25:18-04:00","changed":"2026-08-29T10:22:09-04:00"}
{"id":"flaky-tests","ord":"a7","title":"flaky tests — the ledger of tests found flaky on master that no PR owns","desc":"THE LEDGER, not the law: one entry per test file, sightings and dispositions as props on each entry. THE LAW lives in the boot chain — dag-pr-ci's FLAKE POLICY (filed here, dispatched immediately, pi or grok never Opus, one PR when several are open, auto-merge on green). The CI watcher (roster: claude-ci) is the reporting channel; the orchestrator files what it reports.","created":"2026-08-22T11:51:06-04:00","changed":"2026-08-29T10:21:35-04:00"}
{"id":"awaiting-word","ord":"a8","title":"Awaiting the human's word","desc":"The orchestrator's surfaced-but-unruled ledger, per orch-findings-inbox: each child names a proposal or observation and the word it awaits. Ruled children get their outcome written and are trashed or promoted to a lane.","created":"2026-08-29T11:40:15-04:00"}
{"id":"design-decisions","ord":"a9","title":"design decisions — agents' judgment calls awaiting the human's review","desc":"Choices agents made on their own, for your review — ONLY two kinds reach this queue (your ruling, 2026-08-29 20:15): (1) decisions with USER-FACING impact (what you see, how it behaves, where your data/config lives); (2) creation or non-creation of packages/*. Everything lower-level stays in the PR body's ## Design decisions section for reviewers and never lands here. One parent per PR, one child per qualifying choice, plain words. Reviewed: fine → trash; wrong → a lane to reverse it.","created":"2026-08-29T17:29:00-04:00","changed":"2026-08-29T20:17:08-04:00"}
{"id":"deferral-437-phone","parent":"deferrals","ord":"a9","title":"olai#437: the flip's composition on a phone (two TARGET strips in one bar) — a look-to-answer","todo":true,"desc":"Accepted under the overnight mandate (minor, a genuine look-first question): how the filter bar composes on a phone now that it carries two touch-target strips. Look at it on your phone and rule.","created":"2026-08-29T22:07:26-04:00","custom":{"node":"done-visible-toggle","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/437"}}
{"id":"await-sitting-8","parent":"awaiting-word","ord":"a0","title":"The eighth sitting's conclusion awaits ratification (+ the recall-shape choice)","todo":true,"desc":"The consolidated conclusion sits at debates/lowy-electricity-2026-08-31/README.md (untracked until ratified; no PR exists — a debate, not a lane). Two words owed: (1) ratify — then it files to projects/olai/lowy-electricity/debate-2026-08-31.md and the roadmap absorbs the deepened roster design + the ledgered conditions; (2) the recall shape — #165 returns as plugin-recall with the contributed-matcher hook, or core-first with the hook population-gated, or decided at the PR's own review.","created":"2026-08-31T10:48:02-04:00","custom":{"node":"lowy-sitting-8"}}
{"id":"dd-449","parent":"design-decisions","ord":"a0","title":"olai#449 (file delete): the page grows a Delete face beside Edit, with one confirming question","todo":true,"desc":"User-facing calls the author made, for your review: a document page (and an emptied outline's page) grows a Delete control beside Edit, behind one confirming question that names the path; the refusal for a non-empty outline names its records; git is the stated undo (no in-app inverse); delete is deliberately NOT in the MCP apply batch (single-op only, pinned by test). Fine → trash this entry; wrong → a lane reverses it.","created":"2026-08-31T13:08:34-04:00","custom":{"node":"no-file-delete-op","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/449"}}
{"id":"dd-453-dial","parent":"design-decisions","ord":"a1","title":"olai#453: the kolu dial keeps its own package","todo":true,"desc":"The fold's first open question, ruled by the author with measurement rather than preference. `kolu-client` — the padi client, detect, `koluHalf` — stays its own package instead of folding into the plugin.\n\nTheir argument: the compiler today proves exactly one thing, that the dial names no olai package. A file-level sweep could match that in COVERAGE but not in KIND — every sweep needs a vacuity floor, and a resolution failure cannot be allowed to fail open. Folding it would also make `@olai/tests`' fake-padi import a general package importing a plugin, which costs either a fence carve-out or a fourth registry door. So: two packages per appliance, which the seventh lowy sitting called the honest end state and which odu already is — and `odu-client`'s placement comes out consistent rather than inconsistent.\n\nReaches you because it is a non-creation of a `packages/*`, which is the class the dag says you rule on. Review is asynchronous and never a merge gate: ratify and this is trashed, overturn and it becomes a lane.","created":"2026-08-31T21:49:39-04:00","custom":{"node":"appliance-fold","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/453"}}
{"id":"dd-453-interface","parent":"design-decisions","ord":"a2","title":"olai#453: the plugin interface moves to packages/plugin-api","todo":true,"desc":"The fold's second open question. `@olai/plugins` becomes `@olai/plugin-api` at `packages/plugin-api/`, so `packages/plugins/` is purely the tenant container and the fence's graph walk stops reading as a contradiction. The tenants are `packages/plugins/olai-plugin-kolu` and `packages/plugins/olai-plugin-odu`, package names unscoped `olai-plugin-*` because the repo's one naming law is directory == package name.\n\nThe author argued this against the nested `packages/plugins/interface/` alternative in the PR body.\n\nReaches you because it CREATES a `packages/*`, which is the class the dag says you rule on. Asynchronous — never a merge gate.","created":"2026-08-31T21:49:39-04:00","custom":{"node":"appliance-fold","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/453"}}
{"id":"flake-deliveries","parent":"flaky-tests","ord":"a0","title":"packages/chat/src/deliveries.test.ts is flaky on master","todo":true,"desc":"Found in passing by #453's author and deliberately NOT fixed there (a fix smuggled into a packaging move is what the scope fence forbids). Their measurement: 9 runs on the fold's head gave 7 pass / 2 fail; 4 runs on master at 12b3b6ad gave 3 pass / 1 fail — same two cases, same stream error both times. So the flake predates the fold and no PR owns it, which is what puts it here.\n\nWORTH YOUR EYE BEYOND THE LEDGER: this is the DOORBELL's own delivery test, from #450 — the mechanism that wakes this conversation on fleet events. A flaky test over the waking path is the one flake that can hide a real fault in the thing that tells me anything is wrong.\n\nThe flake policy says dispatch immediately, no triage lag, commodity author never Opus. Your pacing ruling tonight says strictly one lane at a time. I have QUEUED it rather than dispatched it — the specific instruction of the evening beats the standing default — and it needs your word on which governs when the two disagree.","created":"2026-08-31T21:49:39-04:00","custom":{"node":"appliance-fold","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/453"}}
{"id":"await-claude-weekly-limit","parent":"awaiting-word","ord":"a1","title":"Claude's weekly limit is at 94% with three Opus lanes queued","todo":true,"desc":"Read off the CI watcher's own banner when it spawned at 2026-09-01 03:40 UTC: **94% of the weekly limit used, resetting Sep 2 at 1am America/New_York** — about 25 hours out. Every Claude seat draws on it: the fold's author (Opus), the CI watcher (Sonnet), and every Opus reviewer.\n\n**Why it needs your word.** You ruled \"use claude for 3/5\", so three of the four queued lanes carry `agent-claude-opus` — the self-killing server, odu's lying `wait --settle`, and the adapter bump. At 94% with a full day before reset, those three are unlikely to all get through, and a lane that dies mid-implement leaves a half-written worktree rather than a clean stop.\n\n**What the orchestrator did NOT do:** reassign them. Your instruction named Claude, and the roster says your word beats the tier rule. The options, for you to pick at breakfast:\n1. Leave it — lanes stall at the limit and resume after the reset.\n2. Run ONE Opus lane (the self-killing server has the strongest Opus case: kernel truths tests cannot pin) and put the other two back on commodity authors.\n3. Put all three back on commodity authors with Opus reviewing, which is the tier rule's own default.\n\nThe fold itself is nearly clear of the limit — its remaining Claude work is the Sonnet CI watcher; its reviewers are grok and pi.","created":"2026-08-31T23:37:56-04:00","custom":{"node":"appliance-fold","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/453"}}
{"id":"await-unsupervised-night","parent":"awaiting-word","ord":"a2","title":"A lane sat dead for six and a half hours and nothing noticed","todo":true,"desc":"**What happened.** The `mcp-mirrors-invisible` lane's author (pi, terminal 83892b49) died at 04:27 UTC on `Retry failed after 3 attempts: Connection error` — the kimi pool outage the roster names as one of pi's two known failure shapes. It had finished the implementation and a successful nix build; nothing was committed or pushed. The lane then sat dead until 10:57 UTC. Six and a half hours of the night produced nothing.\n\n**Why the doorbell did not save it.** It rang — every ten minutes, about forty times. The whole batch reached the conversation AT ONCE at 10:57 rather than as each was sent, which is the accumulate-and-deliver-on-reopen behaviour documented for a conversation whose session is not live. So the orchestrator did not ignore forty calls; it received one message containing forty. The distinction matters for diagnosis and not at all for the outcome.\n\n**The hole this exposes.** Every recovery path in the pipeline assumes the orchestrator is reachable when an event fires. When it is not, there is no floor: no agent notices its own death, no step times out, and a dispatched lane can burn a whole night silently. The heartbeat proves the WATCHER is alive; nothing proves the ORCHESTRATOR is consuming what it sends.\n\n**Not proposing a process change** — that is the human's to rule (the ban). What is worth their word: whether a lane that has been `doing` with an idle terminal for longer than some bound should be escalated by something other than the orchestrator, since the orchestrator is exactly the component that was missing.\n\n**Recovery, done:** a fresh pi (0117e874) resumed in the same worktree with a resume pointer, told to read the diff and judge it rather than trust it. The pool is back up; it is working.","created":"2026-09-01T06:58:33-04:00","changed":"2026-09-01T06:58:33-04:00","custom":{"node":"mcp-mirrors-invisible"}}
